# fastlane, pinned.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# .github/workflows/testflight.yml used to call bare `fastlane` — whichever
# version the macos-26 image happened to ship that week. The `beta` lane leans
# on version-specific gym behaviour (it hands xcodebuild its own
# -authenticationKey* flags on xcargs because gym passes the api_key to the
# export step only, and it reads SharedValues::IPA_OUTPUT_PATH back out), so a
# runner image that rolls fastlane forward can break a release with no commit of
# ours in between. This file, `bundle exec fastlane beta`, and the lockfile are
# what hold it still.
#
# 2.238.0 is the version the image ships today, so pinning it changes nothing
# about the builds we have been making — it only stops the next roll.
#
# THE LOCKFILE
# Gemfile.lock pins fastlane's own dependency graph and must be committed. It
# can only be generated where bundler runs, which is not the Linux box these
# agents work on:
#
#   cd ios && bundle install && git add Gemfile.lock
#
# Until it exists, ruby/setup-ruby's bundler-cache runs `bundle lock` itself and
# the transitive versions still float; once it does, bundler runs in deployment
# mode and a Gemfile edit without a matching lock fails the job.
source "https://rubygems.org"

gem "fastlane", "2.238.0"
